+# Daily Grind Web App - Security Audit & Policy Deployment Report
+
+## 1. Executive Summary
+
+An audit of the Daily Grind web application was performed to identify security risks and implement preventative measures for safely deploying Content Security Policy (CSP) and HTTP security policies. Prior to this intervention, the server emitted zero security headers, lacked CSP violation telemetry, left client-side storage unpurged upon logout, and allowed arbitrary HTTP methods.
+
+Defensive controls have been implemented to safely deploy security policies using dual-mode CSP (Enforced + Report-Only telemetry), modern Reporting API endpoints, post-logout storage sanitization via `Clear-Site-Data`, and HTTP verb restrictions.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Identified Vulnerabilities & Security Gaps
+
+| ID | Category | Description | Severity | Remediated |
+|---|---|---|:---:|:---:|
+| **SEC-01** | Content Security Policy | No CSP was defined. Vulnerable to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), malicious script injection, and unsafe frame embeddings. | High | Yes |
+| **SEC-02** | CSP Safe Deployment Telemetry | No mechanism existed to monitor policy violations or assess rule regressions without risking site breakage. | High | Yes |
+| **SEC-03** | Session & Cache Residue | `/logout` did not purge client-side cached data, local storage, or session identifiers. | Medium | Yes |
+| **SEC-04** | Clickjacking / UI Redressing | Absence of `X-Frame-Options` and `frame-ancestors` allowed arbitrary third-party framing. | Medium | Yes |
+| **SEC-05** | MIME-Sniffing | Missing `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` allowed user-agent MIME-type sniffing vulnerabilities. | Low | Yes |
+| **SEC-06** | Referrer Leakage | Absence of explicit `Referrer-Policy` could expose full URLs containing paths to cross-origin targets. | Low | Yes |
+| **SEC-07** | Browser Feature Exposure | Powerful device APIs (camera, microphone, geolocation, usb) were not restricted via `Permissions-Policy`. | Low | Yes |
+| **SEC-08** | HTTP Verb Tampering | Server accepted arbitrary HTTP methods without restriction. | Low | Yes |
+
+---
+
+## 3. Preventative Measures Implemented
+
+### 3.1 Content Security Policy & Safe Deployment Mode
+- **Dual Policy Deployment:**
+  - Implemented `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only` alongside `Content-Security-Policy` to monitor policy impacts and safely stage future restrictions.
+  - Configured directive allowances:
+    - `default-src 'self'`: Restricts fallback resource loads to same-origin.
+    - `script-src 'self'`: Disallows inline scripts and untrusted external scripts.
+    - `style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'`: Safely permits the application's internal CSS.
+    - `img-src 'self' https://images.unsplash.com https: data:`: Accommodates Unsplash hero photography and web stock photos while barring unauthorized origins.
+    - `frame-ancestors 'self'`: Prevents cross-origin framing/clickjacking.
+    - `base-uri 'self'` and `form-action 'self'`: Prevents base URI hijacking and form action redirection.
+    - `report-uri /csp-report` and `report-to csp-endpoint`: Ingests violation reports for active auditing.
+
+### 3.2 Violation Reporting Endpoints
+- Deployed `/csp-report` (along with `/reports` and `/api/csp-report`) in `server.js` accepting `application/csp-report`, `application/reports+json`, and `application/json` payloads.
+- Added payload size limiting (64 KB ceiling) and stream destruction to protect against denial-of-service (DoS) memory exhaustion.
+- Supports preflight `OPTIONS` with appropriate `Allow: POST, OPTIONS` headers.
+
+### 3.3 Post-Logout Sanitization
+- Configured `Clear-Site-Data: "cache", "cookies", "storage"` on `/logout` to reliably clear client state and prevent session residue upon user logout.
+
+### 3.4 HTTP Security Headers
+- `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`: Prevents MIME confusion attacks.
+- `X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN`: Backwards-compatible clickjacking defense.
+- `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains`: Enforces HTTPS transport.
+- `Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin`: Minimizes referrer leakage across origins.
+- `Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()`: Blocks unused browser hardware APIs.
+- `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin` & `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin`: Browser context isolation.
+- `X-XSS-Protection: 0`: Disables legacy flawed browser XSS filters in favor of CSP.
+
+### 3.5 Method Handling & Markup Hardening
+- Method enforcement: Enforces `GET` and `HEAD` for static content, returning `405 Method Not Allowed` for unsupported HTTP verbs.
+- Replaced inline style attribute in `index.html` (`style="text-align: center;"`) with a structured CSS class `.text-center`.
+- Added `<meta name="referrer" content="strict-origin-when-cross-origin">` to `index.html`.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Verification
+
+A suite of automated integration tests (`test/security.test.js`) verifies:
+- Response headers on static pages (`/`)
+- CSP directives and reporting endpoints
+- Logout cleanup (`Clear-Site-Data`)
+- Violation report ingestion (`POST /csp-report`)
+- Method restrictions (`TRACE -> 405`, `HEAD -> 200`)
